lique, then a description of a generating set and Gröbner bases of 𝐼𝐺 can be given in terms of the original graph.\n\nIn [33], the authors also discussed applications to algebraic statistics by relating cut ideals to binary graph models, Markov random fields and phylogenetic models on split systems as a generalization of binary Jukes–Cantor models. From an algebraic perspective, one of the most important aspects of the aforementioned paper is a list of conjectures.\n\nMore precisely, in [33, Conjecture 3.5] it is conjectured that 𝐼𝐺 is generated in degree at most two if and only if 𝐺 is a 𝐾4-minor-free graph. Such graphs are closely related to series–parallel graphs. A second conjecture states that 𝐼𝐺 is generated in degree at most 4 if and only if 𝐺 is 𝐾5-minor-free; see [33, Conjectures 3.6]. Note that in algebraic statistics, the highest degree in a minimal system of generators of the toric ideal 𝐼𝐺 is also known as the Markov width; see, e.g., [2]. It is also conjectured that 𝕂⁡[𝐺] is normal and Cohen–Macaulay respectively if and only if 𝐺 is 𝐾5-minor-free; see [33, Conjectures 3.7]. Observe that the “only if” part of all conjectures have been verified in [33].\n\nRecently these conjectures as well as some related questions were studied by many authors, e.g., in [[5], [8], [16], [25], [27], [28], [30], [31]]. Note that Engström gave an affirmative answer to the first conjecture in [16]. The other conjectures are still open. \nCorollary 6.4\n\nThe set of connected graphs 𝐺 with 𝜇⁡(𝐼𝐺) ≤𝑘 is combinatorial retract-closed for any 𝑘.\n\nAs another consequence of Corollary 6.3, we can classify those connected graphs whose cut ideals are generated in a single degree.\n\nCorollary 6.5\n\nLet 𝐺 be a connected and non-complete graph. Then 𝐼𝐺 has a generator of degree 2. In particular, the following statements are equivalent:\n\n  * (a)\n\n𝐼𝐺 is generated in a single degree;\n\n  * (b)\n\n𝐼𝐺 is generated in degree 2;\n\n  * (c)\n\n𝐺 is 𝐾4-minor-free.\n\nProof\n\nSince 𝐺 is non-complete and connected, it follows that it has an induced subgraph isomorphic to 𝑃3, say with vertices {𝑢,𝑣,𝑤}. Without loss of generality, we denote that induced subgraph by 𝑃3, and also assume that deg𝑃3⁡(𝑣) =2. It follows from Corollary 5.8 that the induced subgraph 𝑃3 is indeed a neighborhood-minor of 𝐺. Thus, by Corollary 6.3 (c), we deduce that 𝐼𝐺 has a minimal generator of degree 2, because 𝐼𝑃3 is generated in degree 2 by [25, Corollary 4.3] (see also [33, Example 2.3]). \nProof\n\nThe implication (b) ⇒ (a) follows from Example 7.1, Example 7.4, namely, 𝐼𝑃3 and 𝐼𝐾2⁢♯𝐾1⁢𝐾3 have 2-linear resolutions, and 𝐼𝐾4 has a 4-linear resolution.\n\nIt remains to prove (a) ⇒(b). Assume that 𝐼𝐺 has a 𝑑-linear resolution with 𝑑 ≥2. Then 𝐼𝐺 is generated in a single degree 𝑑 ≥2. On the one hand, by Proposition 3.1, it follows that 𝐺 ≠𝐾2,𝐾3. On the other hand, by Corollary 6.5 and Remark 6.6, it follows that 𝐺 is either 𝐾4 or 𝐾4-minor-free. We need to show that for any 𝐾4-minor-free graph, which is not isomorphic to 𝑃3 or 𝐾2⁢♯𝐾1⁢𝐾3, the ideal 𝐼𝐺 does not have a linear resolution. Let |𝑉| =𝑛. We may assume that 𝑛 ≥4, since the only connected graphs with 𝑛 <4 and |𝐸| ≥1, are 𝐾2, 𝐾3 and 𝑃3.\n\nFirst suppose that 𝑛 =4. Then according to Table 1, we only need to consider 𝐺 =𝑃4,𝐾1,3,𝐶4,𝐾4 ∖𝑒. In the first two cases, which are trees with three edges, by [25, Corollary 4.3] it follows that their cut ideals are generated in degree 𝑑 =2. By [25, Proposition 4.4] we have that reg𝑆𝐺⁡(𝐼𝐺) =4, which implies that 𝐼𝐺 does not have a 2-linear resolution. In the cases where 𝐺 =𝐶4 or 𝐺 =𝐾4 ∖𝑒, we know, by Theorem 6.9 and Proposition 3.3, that 𝑆𝐺 /𝐼𝐺 is a complete intersection with projective dimension 3 or 2. This implies that 𝛽𝑆𝐺1,4⁡(𝐼𝐺) ≠0, since in both cases 𝐺 is a ring graph. Hence, by [25, Theorem 6.2] the ideal 𝐼𝐺 is generated by quadrics. Thus, we deduce that 𝐼𝐺 does not have a linear resolution. Therefore, the only graphs with 𝑛 ≤4 whose cut ideals have linear resolutions are 𝑃3, 𝐾2⁢♯𝐾1⁢𝐾3 and 𝐾4.\n\nNote that, in Corollary 6.5, cut ideals with property 𝑁0 have been classified. It is natural to ask whether the property 𝑁𝑝 can be characterized in our setting. We concentrate here on the property 𝑁1. Note that an ideal satisfying property 𝑁1 is also called linearly presented.\n\nA consequence of Corollary 6.3 is the following necessary condition for satisfying the property 𝑁1.\n\nCorollary 6.15\n\nLet 𝐺 =(𝑉,𝐸) be a graph with |𝐸| ≥1, and assume that 𝐼𝐺 satisfies property 𝑁1. Then 𝐺 is a connected (𝐾4,𝐾4∖𝑒,𝐶4)-combinatorial retract-free graph.\n\nProof\n\nSince 𝐼𝐺 satisfies property 𝑁1, it follows by definition that 𝐼𝐺 is generated by quadrics. Hence, 𝐺 is connected and 𝐾4-minor-free by Proposition 6.7 and Corollary 6.5, respectively. Then, by Remark 6.2, the graph 𝐺 is 𝐾4-combinatorial retract-free.\n\nWe have that 𝛽𝑆𝐾4∖𝑒1,4⁡(𝐼𝐾4∖𝑒) ≠0 and 𝛽𝑆𝐶41,4⁡(𝐼𝐶4) ≠0, because the cut algebras of 𝐾4 ∖𝑒 and 𝐶4 are complete intersections by Theorem 6.9, and their cut ideals, which are not principal ideals by Proposition 3.3, are generated by quadrics by Corollary 6.5. Thus, if 𝐺 has a combinatorial retract isomorphic to 𝐾4 ∖𝑒 or 𝐶4, then Corollary 6.3 (a) implies that 𝛽𝑆𝐺1,4⁡(𝐼𝐺) ≠0, a contradiction to having property 𝑁1. Hence, 𝐺 is (𝐾4,𝐾4∖𝑒,𝐶4)-combinatorial retract-free.  □\n\nWe would like to end this section by posing the following problem which is verified by computations for all graphs up to 5 vertices; see Table 1.\n\nProblem 6.16\n\nWhat is the complete characterization of cut ideals of graphs which satisfy property 𝑁1? Is the necessary condition given in Corollary 6.15 a sufficient condition as well?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCut ideals of K4-minor free graphs are generated (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=34a1d000fd7514d3338400c12b9114185e63f0dd&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Cut ideals ... The cut ideal ... The largest degree of